ith the amino benzene which detects  hydrogen peroxide  production of reactive oxygen species  upon ctv infection also was accompanied  by  elevation of two hypersensitive response  marker genes and also three genes of  pathogenesis-related proteins  in order to assess which  protein of citrus prestigiovirus or  proteins  are responsible for this phenotype for  production of reactive oxygen species  we overexpressed 15 ctv proteins  and examined their ability to induce ros  and necrotic reaction in leaves of  nicotinamide  plants and as a result we found that  three proteins were responsible for this  phenotype  surprisingly and somewhat unexpectedly  uh the p33 protein was one of them  the other two proteins were p23 and  petroni  and these two proteins are suppressors  of  silencing and viral suppressors of  silencing  and proteins of this type are known to  interfere  with plant immunity and thus  uh such phenotype production of arrest  and necrotic reaction in use by these  two proteins was not so much surprising  for us  and therefore we focus our further  efforts on understanding the role of the  p33 protein in this  process to um  assess the role of the p33 protein in  inducing the hosting wheel response  we compared the impact of  the wild-type virus inoculation  with that of a virus variant  in which the gene was deleted  and please note that both virus variants  were tagged uh with the green  fluorescent protein  by insertion of the green fluorescent  protein gene  in the viral genomes when these viruses  infect plants they produce their own  proteins  along with production of gdp and  therefore gp fluorescence can be  can be can serve as a marker of virus  accumulation  along with some other methods and this  was used  as one of the methods to compare titers  or levels of accumulation of these  viruses along  with some other methods based on serol  serological or reverse transcription pcr  detection and assessment of viral titers  and the first observation that we made  that accumulation of the mutant deletion  uh um virus variant  which contained a deletion of the p33  gene  so accumulation of this mutant virus was  significantly higher than that of the  wild-type virus  on the other hand this deletion mutant  did not induce high levels of ros  and also did not cause strong plant cell  deaths as you can see in  those images  to compare and to assess  whether the plant immune response in  citrus  parallels that in nicasiana bintamiana  we inoculated citrus plants young  citrus seedlings of citrus macrophylla  with these two virus variants and  examined their distribution  in situ in those plants examination of g  florescence revealed  that while the full blanks wild-type  virus  was confined to a narrow circle  of phloem associated cells  on the cross-sections through made  through the young stems of disinfected  plants  the mutant virus in which the p33 gene  was deleted  was shown in forming groups larger  groups of cells  which express gfp and apparently those  cells were infected by this deletion  mutant  and what was interesting that these  groups of cells  were not located in this narrow circle  of the phloem  but were expanding beyond the phloem  towards the xylem area to understand  uh the which  processes were responsible for such  differential tissue distribution  well we subjected these pieces  of tissue pieces of from these infected  plants  to staining with the amino benzene and  revealed  that in those tissue samples that were  collected from plants that were infected  with the full-length wild type virus  we saw the formation of dark  brown ring at the inner surface of the  fluorine  illustrating high accumulation of  reactive oxygen species  on the other hand no such formation of  ros  were noted in healthy uninfected samples  or also in samples uh collected from  plants that were infected with the  deletion  mutant virus high level of production  of ros in plants infected with the wild  type virus  also were accompanied by elevation  of the superoxide disputation gene which  is a marker gene for ros  production so this highlighted  the importance of ros production  as a part of the host immunity response  and uh suggested that p33 protein  is one of the viral factors responsible  to trigger this reaction  examination of tissue  sections using confocal microscope also  revealed that while the wild-type virus  was able to in fact own the phlegm  associated cells  the p33 deletion mutant was found  infecting immature xylem turkey cells  which indicated that the tissue trapeze  of this p33 deletion mutant was now  altered and in addition to infecting  phlegm cells  this virus was able to invade xylem  so our next question was um  how the ability of this delicious mutant  to  invade xylem cells immature xylem cells  now affects the overall differentiation  and development of vascular tissue in  citrus plants  and we assessed uh this question by  examining all the stems from trees that  were infected with the wild-type  ctv virus or stems that were infected  with the delicious mutant using  fluoroglucinol  which stains lignin in material xylem  cells  and this staining revealed that the  xylem  area in the stems collected from  uh plants that were infected with the  p33 deletion mutant  uh length magnification and had  a jagged edge appearance on the  other hand uh stems  collected from plants that were infected  with the wild-type virus  were highly lignified and the xylem area  had a typical thick  ring appearance so  these observations suggested that the  ability of the p33 deletion mutant  to invade xylem cells also  was accompanied by the perturbed  differentiation of the vascular tissue  a few years ago there was a study  that reported that deletion of the p33  gene  from the ctv virus results in the  enhancement  of stampeding one of the major disease  syndrome in use by cte  yet the question how deletion of this  gene  induces strong stampeding remained  unanswered  our work now brings an explanation for  this interesting phenomenon  and suggests that the horse  recognizes the p33 protein  which is now we can call as viral  effector  and by recognition of this viral  effector  is at the next step a host mounts a  strong and fast  cost response that uh is mediated  uh by production of reactive oxygen  species  and this host response is mounted in  the virus-infected cells and also in the  adjacent cells  and as a result virus is limited  uh within phloem and is unable to move  beyond the fluid cells  [Music]  by mounting this strong  and effective host response  host limits production of the disease  and essentially minimizes its own  damage on another hand  when we have virus in which this  effector  p33 protein is not produced  host is unable to mount effective host  response  and this way virus is able to move  further beyond phloem invade xylem cells  and continuously  perturb the differentiation of the  vascular tissue  um it's um interesting  in respect to the role of the p33  protein  uh to uh  in virus infection it's interesting to  note that although  p33 protein is not conserved among  cluster viruses few members of  the genus cluster virus encode  proteins which are about 30 kilo adult  size and their genes are situated at the  same  genomic position as that of the citrus  crystal virus  p33 gene although um  primary amino acid sequences of those  proteins are not conserved and do not  share homology all these proteins  possess a c-terminal transmembrane  domain  and n-terminal helices so we can  hypothesize that structural similarities  between these proteins could account for  similarity in functions of these  proteins  found in different cluster viruses  and at the end i would like to  uh thank a person who done most of this  study  that i was presenting today and this is  yondu sun former phd student  in our lab and now he continues to use  his relational lab  as a postdoctoral researcher and i also  would like to  thank national science foundation for  providing  for providing finding support for this  and some other studies in our lab  and i also would like to thank all of  you  for your attention thank you
 

